1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,072
Salt vanishes in water.

2
00:00:02,072 --> 00:00:03,644
Chalk dust just sinks.

3
00:00:03,644 --> 00:00:06,245
And an old kettle grows a white crust.

4
00:00:06,645 --> 00:00:08,264
Some solids dissolve a lot.

5
00:00:08,264 --> 00:00:09,525
Some hardly dissolve.

6
00:00:09,525 --> 00:00:11,323
Chemistry puts a number on it.

7
00:00:11,773 --> 00:00:14,491
Diya stirs chalk powder
into a glass of water.

8
00:00:14,841 --> 00:00:16,259
She stirs and stirs.

9
00:00:16,259 --> 00:00:18,467
Most of it settles at the bottom.

10
00:00:18,967 --> 00:00:22,997
By the end, you will see how one tiny
number decides when a solid appears.

11
00:00:23,647 --> 00:00:25,706
First, three kinds of salts.

12
00:00:25,706 --> 00:00:30,145
Highly soluble ones dissolve more
than zero point one mole per litre.

13
00:00:30,495 --> 00:00:34,737
Slightly soluble ones, zero
point zero one to zero point one.

14
00:00:35,137 --> 00:00:38,814
Sparingly soluble ones, less
than zero point zero one.

15
00:00:38,814 --> 00:00:40,391
Today is about these.

16
00:00:40,991 --> 00:00:45,596
On our molecule bench, solid barium
sulphate sits at the bottom of a beaker

17
00:00:45,596 --> 00:00:46,496
of water.

18
00:00:46,743 --> 00:00:50,028
A few barium ions and sulphate
ions break away into the water.

19
00:00:50,428 --> 00:00:53,054
Some bump into the
solid and stick back on.

20
00:00:53,054 --> 00:00:55,430
Leaving and returning, again and again.

21
00:00:55,830 --> 00:00:59,981
When both happen at the same
speed, the water holds all it can.

22
00:00:59,981 --> 00:01:01,173
It is saturated.

23
00:01:01,573 --> 00:01:03,778
The number of ions stops changing.

24
00:01:03,778 --> 00:01:05,343
This is an equilibrium.

25
00:01:05,943 --> 00:01:10,179
Its constant is called the
solubility product, K s p.

26
00:01:10,529 --> 00:01:12,762
Multiply the ion concentrations.

27
00:01:12,762 --> 00:01:16,769
Leave the solid out, because
a pure solid never changes.

28
00:01:17,169 --> 00:01:22,130
For barium sulphate, K s p is one
point one times ten to the minus ten,

29
00:01:22,130 --> 00:01:24,111
at two ninety eight kelvin.

30
00:01:24,111 --> 00:01:26,237
Tiny number, tiny solubility.

31
00:01:26,887 --> 00:01:29,332
Now link K s p to solubility.

32
00:01:29,332 --> 00:01:32,952
We call the solubility
s, in moles per litre.

33
00:01:33,302 --> 00:01:34,202
Step one.

34
00:01:34,153 --> 00:01:36,728
For a salt AB, s moles dissolve.

35
00:01:36,728 --> 00:01:39,906
That gives s of A plus, and s of B minus.

36
00:01:40,656 --> 00:01:41,576
Step two.

37
00:01:41,576 --> 00:01:43,620
K s p equals s times s.

38
00:01:43,620 --> 00:01:45,831
So K s p equals s squared.

39
00:01:46,581 --> 00:01:47,497
Step three.

40
00:01:47,497 --> 00:01:52,075
For barium sulphate, s is the square
root of one point one times ten to

41
00:01:52,075 --> 00:01:53,200
the minus ten.

42
00:01:53,200 --> 00:01:56,793
About one point zero five
times ten to the minus five.

43
00:01:57,543 --> 00:02:02,059
A salt AB two is like one toy box
with two of the same toy inside.

44
00:02:02,409 --> 00:02:05,209
One unit gives one A, and two B.

45
00:02:05,209 --> 00:02:07,732
So s of A, and two s of B.

46
00:02:08,132 --> 00:02:11,151
K s p equals s, times two s squared.

47
00:02:11,151 --> 00:02:12,946
That is four s cubed.

48
00:02:13,346 --> 00:02:17,442
For any salt A x B y, raise
each ion to its count.

49
00:02:17,442 --> 00:02:22,271
K s p equals x to the x, y
to the y, s to the x plus y.

50
00:02:22,871 --> 00:02:24,755
Now the common ion effect.

51
00:02:24,755 --> 00:02:26,211
Common means shared.

52
00:02:26,561 --> 00:02:29,680
We add sodium sulphate
to our saturated beaker.

53
00:02:29,680 --> 00:02:33,590
It brings extra sulphate ions,
the same ion our salt has.

54
00:02:33,990 --> 00:02:36,097
Now there are too many sulphate ions.

55
00:02:36,097 --> 00:02:38,305
The product of ions goes above K s p.

56
00:02:38,705 --> 00:02:40,352
So the balance shifts back.

57
00:02:40,352 --> 00:02:43,603
Ions join the solid, and more
barium sulphate falls out.

58
00:02:44,003 --> 00:02:47,003
So a salt dissolves less when
one of its ions is already there.

59
00:02:47,603 --> 00:02:48,516
Now compare.

60
00:02:48,516 --> 00:02:51,057
K s p is the most the water can hold.

61
00:02:51,057 --> 00:02:54,352
The ionic product, Q, is
what it holds right now.

62
00:02:54,752 --> 00:02:58,225
If Q is less than K s
p, more solid dissolves.

63
00:02:58,225 --> 00:03:01,426
If Q equals K s p, the water is saturated.

64
00:03:01,826 --> 00:03:03,025
Pause and think.

65
00:03:03,025 --> 00:03:06,162
If Q is bigger than K
s p, what do you see?

66
00:03:09,362 --> 00:03:10,518
A solid appears!

67
00:03:10,518 --> 00:03:14,793
Extra ions fall out as a precipitate,
until Q comes down to K s p.

68
00:03:15,443 --> 00:03:16,462
Remember this.

69
00:03:16,462 --> 00:03:18,880
Q above K s p, a solid falls out.

70
00:03:18,880 --> 00:03:20,584
Q below, more dissolves.

71
00:03:21,284 --> 00:03:22,974
Now, one worked example.

72
00:03:23,274 --> 00:03:27,642
A salt AB two has K s p four
times ten to the minus twelve.

73
00:03:27,642 --> 00:03:30,039
Find its solubility in pure water.

74
00:03:30,539 --> 00:03:32,735
K s p equals four s cubed.

75
00:03:32,735 --> 00:03:36,426
So s cubed is one times
ten to the minus twelve.

76
00:03:37,176 --> 00:03:38,594
Take the cube root.

77
00:03:38,594 --> 00:03:42,159
s is one times ten to the
minus four moles per litre.

78
00:03:42,909 --> 00:03:45,746
Now add zero point one molar B minus.

79
00:03:45,746 --> 00:03:49,266
K s p equals s times
zero point one, squared.

80
00:03:49,266 --> 00:03:51,311
We ignore the tiny two s.

81
00:03:52,061 --> 00:03:56,667
s is four times ten to the minus
twelve over zero point zero one.

82
00:03:56,667 --> 00:03:59,076
Four times ten to the minus ten.

83
00:03:59,076 --> 00:04:02,015
Two hundred and fifty thousand times less!

84
00:04:02,665 --> 00:04:04,126
Now, the exam trap.

85
00:04:04,476 --> 00:04:08,026
For AB two, students write
K s p equals s times two s.

86
00:04:08,026 --> 00:04:10,071
They forget to square the two s.

87
00:04:10,471 --> 00:04:12,395
Raise each ion to its count.

88
00:04:12,395 --> 00:04:14,855
Two s, squared, is four s squared.

89
00:04:15,505 --> 00:04:16,976
Where is this asked?

90
00:04:16,976 --> 00:04:19,010
Boards ask K s p expressions.

91
00:04:19,010 --> 00:04:22,405
Entrance tests ask solubility,
or if a solid forms.

92
00:04:23,005 --> 00:04:24,326
Exam level now.

93
00:04:24,326 --> 00:04:29,789
A salt MX two has K s p three point
two times ten to the minus eleven.

94
00:04:29,789 --> 00:04:32,958
What is its solubility in moles per litre?

95
00:04:32,958 --> 00:04:34,335
Pause and solve.

96
00:04:39,735 --> 00:04:41,037
The answer is C.

97
00:04:41,037 --> 00:04:43,329
Two times ten to the minus four.

98
00:04:43,829 --> 00:04:45,942
K s p equals four s cubed.

99
00:04:45,942 --> 00:04:50,931
So s cubed is three point two times
ten to the minus eleven over four.

100
00:04:51,431 --> 00:04:54,152
That is eight times ten
to the minus twelve.

101
00:04:54,152 --> 00:04:56,918
Cube root, two times
ten to the minus four.

102
00:04:57,418 --> 00:04:59,036
A took a square root.

103
00:04:59,036 --> 00:05:01,017
D forgot to divide by four.

104
00:05:01,617 --> 00:05:03,157
Here is your recap.

105
00:05:03,157 --> 00:05:04,739
Screenshot this card.

106
00:05:07,939 --> 00:05:10,125
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

107
00:05:10,525 --> 00:05:11,425
One.

108
00:05:11,003 --> 00:05:13,435
Why is the solid left out of K s p?

109
00:05:14,235 --> 00:05:15,135
Two.

110
00:05:14,725 --> 00:05:17,982
Write K s p for an AB
two salt in terms of s.

111
00:05:18,782 --> 00:05:19,682
Three.

112
00:05:19,302 --> 00:05:21,005
When does a precipitate form?

113
00:05:21,905 --> 00:05:25,592
Next time: why does an iron
gate turn brown and flaky?

114
00:05:25,592 --> 00:05:28,197
We find out in oxidation and reduction.
